COMMENTS.COMMENT:
comm/syslog init (syslog.c): irq_vec_base_set(0) -> mtvec=0x700d4580|2;
mie.MTIE on, mstatus.MIE on; comm_init_wait_msg() = mailbox init + open eps
(9b6e ctrl-rx w/ handler mbox_rx_irq_handler, 9b6c sync) + recv 12-byte timestamp;
snprintf "<%llu>" + append payload + strlen check. Banner over syslog.

main: platform_info_get() (const 0xFFFFFFFFD0000000); irq_table_init();
comm_init_and_banner() (mailbox init, ep open, syslog banner); then infinite wfi.
All work IRQ-driven: encode_work_poll() on pending_encode_tick, commands via mailbox.

avc_stream.c: memset ctx (0xb8) + enc_reg_image (0x70); copy ~40 fields from
0x4c-byte host params (w/h -> ctx+0x2c/0x2e, MB dims (w+15)>>4 -> +0x30/0x31,
fps 0x4b0/fps -> +0x90, profile flags +0xa0..0xa9); assert profile<0x20;
rc init (rc_default_init, rc_set_bitrate, qp min/max/step, mode, ME window 3eb0);
bw_init; enc_start_trigger (0x700b0794=1); set frame-state ctx+0x58..0x60.

Per-frame qp: enabled gate; fps-scaled target; I-frame drift resets;
mode 3: drift +-1..5 qp on 0x9490, +-10 clamp vs 0x9488; rc_calc_qp();
repeat-qp streak 0x948c/0x94a2.

Bitrate tiers -> qp (>=1.5M:31, 1.2M:33, 1M:35, 0.8M:37, 0.5M:39, 0.3M:41, 0.2M:42);
mode 3: geometric qp step (*0x3bb>>10 = *0.9346^k) capped +6/-3;
0x9474=bitrate; 0x94ac = bitrate*fps/600 (128-bit __udivti3); rescale accumulators.

flash_partition_write(chip, addr, data, len): last bound before the SPI controller.
if (chip && data && len && addr <= (int)g_spi_flash_size && len + (addr & 0xffffffff) <= g_spi_flash_size) return spi_flash_program_range(addr & 0xffffffff, data, len, 0);
Bounds to the WHOLE chip (g_spi_flash_size), NOT to any partition. Caller fm_flash_write already passed (long)(int)flash_location, so a 64-bit address with high bits set is sign-extended from its low 32 bits; values whose low32 has bit31 set become negative and are rejected here, but any low32 in [0, g_spi_flash_size) is accepted.

Json_ParseValue(Json_Node*, ParseCtx*): recursive DOM parser; one recursion per '['/'{', bounded ONLY by ctx[3] < 1000 (no stack budget).

FRAME = 160 (0xa0): sp+0 local_a0, sp+8..71 local_98[64] (attacker number-token chars), sp+72 guarded canary, sp+88..159 saved s7..s0,ra.

CONTROLLED BYTES: the number branch copies at most 0x3f chars through the mask DAT_ram_b01f5008 -> alphabet {+,-,.,0-9,e,E} only (ASCII 0x2b..0x65), then NUL + strtod (FUN_ram_b01c4f02). Only a number *leaf* frame writes local_98; '['/'{' frames leave it stale. By placing a number as a sibling at each nesting level an attacker can put a 64-byte ASCII-only window at every depth (160-byte stride, 40% coverage) below the task stack.

EXPLOIT VERDICT = RULED OUT for in-process control-flow hijack (DoS only):
 (1) clean return hijack blocked: canary is TRNG-randomised per boot (stack_canary_init_trng ram:b0280a2a) and checked on every return;
 (2) window bytes are ASCII number chars only -> cannot forge a 64-bit code or heap pointer (no ASLR though, addresses fixed);
 (3) task stacks and Json nodes share the heap_5_ext pool (pvPortMalloc ram:b00c43ea / ctx[4]=ram:b00d0c72 select malloc_sram/psram via DAT_ram_b01fa270); free-list next-pointers are XOR-masked with cookie = mcycle(CSR 0xb00) ^ 0x5A5A5A5A5A5A5A5A at init (FUN_ram_b00c3ad4) and the walk range-asserts (heap_5_ext.c:0x111/0x11e) -> out-of-range pointers abort; attacker cannot compute the cookie;
 (4) configCHECK_FOR_STACK_OVERFLOW is on: the switcher at ram:b00ce42a verifies the 4 words at TCB+0x60 (pxStack) == 0xa5a5a5a5 and calls FUN_ram_b00c3aaa ("***STACK OVERFLOW***") which log+ebreak()s, so the first tick/scheduling event after crossing the stack base halts.
Residual (unproven, needs runtime layout): a 64-byte ASCII window or a spilled legit ra/s0 could corrupt an adjacent heap object header below the stack; because the writes are either ASCII or fixed parser pointers (not attacker-chosen code ptrs) this yields allocator assert/crash, not control flow. See task stacks Task_AllocStackAndTcb ram:b00ceed6 / Task_CreateWithEntry ram:b00e27ea.

metal main() wrapper (freedom-metal):
core_init_per_hart(); hart=get_mhartid();
if hart!=0 AND *(u32*)0x402FE004==0xF0F0F0F0 (handoff status block,
bootstub stamps 0xF0F0F0F0 there on handoff failure):
  hart_barrier_5way(1) - synchronize harts 1..4 before parking.
Then nop(); hart_shutdown_and_park(); return 0.

Per-hart init from core.c:
assert hartid<=4 else assert_fail("core.c",0x37,0) (prints "A:core.c:55:");
memcpy(gp-0x4c0, mscratch, 0x24) - copy 0x24-byte boot-data struct
(pointer left in mscratch by hart_wait_go_and_boot);
hart_mailbox_write(hartid, 0) - clear own go mailbox.

clock rate (Hz) for clock id (clkid<10, table clock_desc_table):
divider = clock_get_divider(clkid); if 0 -> 25 MHz fallback;
else (sysctl_pll_freq_mhz() * 1000000) / divider.

Per-hart UART putc with timeout:
if !uart_slot_registered(hart) return 0;
poll uart_tx_idle(hart) up to tries times, delay_us(hart_delay[hart]) between;
then uart_reg_write(hart, 0, ch) -> THR at uart_base_table[hart]+0.
UARTs are 16550-style with 4-byte register stride (LSR=+0x14, THRE|TEMT=0x60).

CStorageElement_ACS::SetReadPosition(ulong pos): stores this+0x228 with NO validation against
capacity_238. Together with linear-mode Read this is the origin of an out-of-range read_position;
fm_flash_read normally catches it (returns -10) unless pos is in the 2^64-window that wraps its check.

CStorageElement_ACS::WriteRaw (ACS vtable slot +0x68).
long __thiscall WriteRaw(ulong offset, void *src, ulong len) { flash_addr = *(u32*)(this->partition + 0x14) + offset; // partition base + caller offset return ProgramFlashRange(this=flash_addr, len, src, FlashPageMergeCallback); }
ESCAPE PRIMITIVE (proven): `offset` is NOT range-checked against the partition size (this->partition+0x10) nor against this->capacity_238. ProgramFlashRange passes partition=0 to fm_flash_write, and fm_flash_write applies the +0x10 size check ONLY when its partition arg != 0. The only remaining guard is check_flash_area_writeable (global table), which in this build is a no-op (all 20 FlashPartition entries are flagged writable and addresses outside every partition are accepted). Result: an attacker-chosen `offset` yields an arbitrary flash write inside g_spi_flash_size, independent of the caller's partition. Callers: CStorageElement::Write (write_position) and CStorageElement_ACS::Fill. write_position is set by SetWritePosition with no validation.

